\section{Make an Offset Mean One Thing}

The lexer recognizes lexemes and supplies tokens to the parser; a
token is not the string of characters it covers \cite{aho2007compilers}.
Design the source interface so that byte coordinates, token indices,
and display coordinates cannot be passed interchangeably. A
lightweight span consists of a source snapshot identity and the
half-open interval \([s,e)\) of byte offsets in that snapshot. It
admits an empty interval at end of file and composes adjacent ranges
without double counting their shared boundary. The same integer
endpoints in a different file or revision need not designate the
same text. Source IDs used by diagnostics should keep the snapshot
available, or make its absence explicit if it has been evicted.

Use the original bytes as the source of truth. For example, the
three strings \texttt{a}, \texttt{é}, and \texttt{a} on a UTF-8
line occupy respectively one, two, and one bytes. The last
\texttt{a} starts at byte 3 and zero-based Unicode scalar column
2. An editor protocol might demand UTF-16 units; a terminal printer
has yet another question when there are combining marks or tabs.
Neither column convention belongs in the stored token. Convert at
the boundary, with a stated policy for invalid byte sequences and
the treatment of CRLF. If spans may be snapped for display,
do not feed the snapped positions back into the parser.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lll}
\hline
Query & Index & Necessary qualifier \\
\hline
Byte to source line & Sorted line starts & Same immutable snapshot \\
Covering lexical token & Sorted token starts & Confirm half-open bounds \\
Overlapping diagnostics & Augmented interval tree & Report all intersections \\
Editor column to byte & Line text and conversion & Agree on coordinate encoding \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\section{Match the Physical Buffer to the Edit Pattern}

A flat immutable byte array offers cheap slicing and excellent
sequential access. It is a sound default for a batch compiler. An
editor might instead keep original text and append buffers, with
pieces in a balanced tree whose internal nodes store subtree byte
counts. Replacing a range splits pieces at its boundaries, splices
new pieces, and changes only a search path and the affected part
of the tree. With balanced structure and path copying, old
revisions can remain readable. Account for the actual splice and
rebalancing work; the fact that a single seek takes logarithmic
time does not make deletion of arbitrarily many pieces free.
Partition-aware cursors are essential: walking a token one byte at
a time with a fresh seek for each byte squanders the index.

In \Cref{fig:visual-421}, a token straddles a piece boundary.
The token's spelling may be materialized or read through a streaming
cursor, but the parser doesn't need two tokens. Conversely, two
adjacent tokens may lie inside a single piece. Physical storage
seams are not lexical facts. A piece tree can also cache line break
counts; for a CRLF policy the count or its combining logic has
to recognize a carriage return in one piece followed by a newline
in the next.

