\section{Restart Lexing from a Justified Checkpoint}

\begin{verbatim}
relex(old, new, edit):
    require new came from old by this one edit
    # Checkpoint is valid only if its confirming reads
    # could not have observed any byte changed by the edit.
    start := safe_restart_at_or_before(old, edit)
    lexer := resume(new.text, start.offset, start.state)
    result := share_prefix_before(old.tokens, start)

    while not lexer.at_end():
        result.append(lexer.next_token())
        p := lexer.offset
        if p < edit.new_end: continue
        q := p - edit.byte_delta
        prior := old.token_boundary_at(q)
        if prior exists and lexer.state == prior.state:
            # From here the suffix bytes and state agree.
            result.append_rebased_suffix(old.tokens, q, edit)
            return finish(result, new.snapshot_id)

    return finish(result, new.snapshot_id)
\end{verbatim}

Here a checkpoint saves all context relevant to future tokens, such
as string mode, interpolation depth, or indentation state. The
algorithm assumes a deterministic lexer that does not use absolute
offsets for lexical decisions unless the needed context is accounted
for in equality. \texttt{finish} owns the EOF marker in this
sketch. Rebased positions must use the new snapshot ID even when
their numerical offsets do not change. If the lexer can emit
zero-width pending tokens, the boundary and state must include the
pending outputs, or the implementation should restrict checkpoints
to locations with none. If lexical recovery emits diagnostics,
their ranges and replay rules are part of the reuse contract as well.

Diekmann's incremental lexer accounts for the lookahead that may
have influenced an earlier token. In a language with both
\texttt{=} and \texttt{==}, inserting \texttt{=} immediately
after the first one can invalidate a token that ended exactly at
the insertion. Restarting at the nearest recorded token boundary
is therefore not always safe. A simple implementation may restart
at the beginning of the file if it cannot certify a nearer point.
An edit that opens a block comment and leaves it open may force
relexing to EOF even if the byte delta is small.

\section{Use Two Indexes for Two Different Overlap Models}

Nonempty tokens in a token stream are disjoint. A binary search
for the last start at most the queried byte, followed by a check
of the end, is sufficient for point lookup. If trivia is kept on
a different channel the selected token may not cover the query;
say whether to return no token or a neighbor. Empty tokens are
anchors, not covering intervals; make the tie-break explicit for
an insertion query at a shared boundary.

Annotations and diagnostics can nest or cross. Suppose records
cover \([0,40)\), \([16,19)\), and \([18,25)\). A search for byte
23 that only tests the most recent start loses the outer record.
Store interval records in a balanced tree keyed by start with a
maximum end for each subtree. In a range overlap query, a subtree
whose maximum end is no greater than the query's start is
irrelevant, and nodes starting at or after the query's end need not
contribute. Version or rebuild the index when its source coordinates
change. If a text edit splits a diagnostic range, assigning it an
offset delta alone does not repair what the message refers to.

Compare incremental results to fresh lexing after each scripted edit.
Include seam-spanning tokens, CRLF split over pieces, and an edit that
changes comment state without changing byte count. Validate token
kinds, canonical payloads, source slices, and diagnostics from the
new revision. A benchmark should also count bytes relexed, bytes
copied during splicing, and old snapshots retained by a diagnostic
window; these measure different costs than the wall-clock time of a
single favorable edit.

\section{Translate Surviving Ranges, Not Every Old Range}

Take an edit that replaces old bytes \([8,11)\) with five bytes.
A token \([1,5)\) wholly before the change keeps its endpoints;
one at \([12,14)\) moves to \([14,16)\). An old token
\([7,12)\), however, overlaps the edit and must not be mapped by
one delta. Even a token disjoint from the edited bytes can need
rechecking if its lexical lookahead touches them. A cursor anchor
at the old boundary needs a separate bias. At an insertion at byte
8, an anchor biased left remains before the insertion and an anchor
biased right follows the inserted bytes. Do not reuse that cursor
policy for a token's end without first checking whether the token
itself survived.

When cached token payloads are relative to a group, suffix reuse
can store a new group base rather than shifting each token. Prefix
and suffix groups should carry their snapshot context through a
wrapper, while absolute spans handed to clients always name the
snapshot. If parents routinely derive one token's span from a
relative base, assert that the sum still fits the snapshot length
and offset type. This matters even on files shorter than the limit
if implementation arithmetic mixes signed edit deltas with unsigned
byte offsets. Exercise a negative delta at the end of a file and
check that EOF remains an empty range at the new length.

Line caches face the same problem. A single extra line near byte
zero shifts every later line number, but not the text of those
lines. A piece index that aggregates line breaks can translate from
bytes to line number by summing counts along a search path. For a
flat array of line starts, defer rebuilding until requested or use
a persistent chunked sequence with relative offsets. Choose based
on how many line lookups occur per edit, and compare the output of
an indexed path to a simple count from the start on boundary cases.
